   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## ❓ Question #1:\n",
    "\n",
    "What are the **trade-offs** of using todo lists for planning? Consider:\n",
    "- When might explicit planning overhead slow things down?\n",
    "- How granular should todo items be?\n",
    "- What happens if the agent creates todos but never completes them?\n",
    "\n",
    "##### Answer:\n",
    "\n",
    "Using Todo lists improve reliability, task organization, and performance overall for complex tasks, but at the cost of increased overhead via API calls and latency. \n",
    "\n",
    "Explicit planning overhead slows down operations when it's utilized for overly-simple or overly-deterministic tasks, or for real-time apps or any apps that have latency requirements.\n",
    "\n",
    "Todo items should have granularity to the extent that they can complete a given task in a \"reasonable\" amount of time, e.g. one session. If the item is too course, the agent becomes confused by the vagueness of the information and can't complete the task. If the item is too finely granular, the agent becomes too busy trying to manage all of the parts leading to increased API calls and looping.\n",
    "\n",
    "When the agent creates todos but never completes them, it's failure time. It causes runnaway error propagation, runnaway api calls, task abandonment, and looping. "
   ]
