    "Key Considerations When Designing Subagent Configurations: Does the specialization justify the additional cost? Tool count needs to be kept low or confusion ensues and efficiency is lost. Is the potential increase in error propagation worth the addition of subagents? Will the task being undertaken require parallel operations? If not then subagents are a bad choice. \n",
    "\n",
    "Subagents should share tools for tasks common to all of them (read, write, tracking todo lists, etc.), and/or when consistency is required. Subagents should have distinct tools for domain-specific skills, as needed to maintain safety and/or privacy, and to reduce tool overload, since only tools that are needed get used. \n",
    "\n",
    "The llm used by the subagent should be determined by the nature of the subagent's task(s). Complex reasoning and logic requires one of the more advanced models. Speed and simplicity need smaller and more cost-efficient models. Coordination/routing/orchestration require models that are designed to delegate. \n",
    "\n",
    "The right granularity for subagent specialization- Subagents should be used when distinct domain expertise is required, when multiple tools need to used, and where the task(s) at hand would require a long time for a person to complete (> 30 minutes). Subagents shouldn't be used for simple tasks that could use already available tools or better prompts.\n"
